      "title": "TCP: Multisegment PDUs don't contain keep-alives",
      "message": "TCP: Multisegment PDUs don't contain keep-alives\n\nPer RFC 9293  3.8.4. TCP Keep-Alives,\n\n\"Keep-alive packets MUST only be sent when no sent data is outstanding\"\nand \"there is no new or unacknowledged data to be sent.\" Keep-alives\nare distinguished from retransmissions and zero window probes.\nThey SHOULD be sent with no data, but for compatibility some network\nstacks are configured to send a garbage octet.\n\nDon't add the garbage octet, if present, to a multi-segment PDU for some\nof the same reasons that we don't add retransmissions.\n\nIn fact, we can go further, as the above means that a multi-segment PDU\ncannot span a keep-alive boundary. (Unlike retransmissions and zero window\nprobes.) If we have a keep-alive, and also an unfinished MSP that started\nat the same sequence number as this keep-alive, then the previous MSP is\nin fact also a keep-alive garbage octet that was sent to the next\ndissector (presumably configured for the port) and incorrectly\ndetermined to be the start of a MSP. This happens if the first packet\nfor a TCP stream in a capture is a keep-alive segment with a garbage\noctet, because we cannot determine that the first segment is a\nkeep-alive with no prior sequence number.\n\nFix #20287\n",
